<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Influenza A (J10.1)</content>. Rapid molecular test positive for influenza A. Uncomplicated influenza within 48 hours of onset in a person whose adrenal insufficiency puts him at higher risk of complications, so antiviral treatment is advised. Lungs clear and saturation normal; no sign of pneumonia. [S]<list><item>Oseltamivir 75 mg twice daily for five days.</item><item>Rest, fluids, and acetaminophen for fever and aches.</item><item>Return or call for breathlessness, chest pain, or fever lasting more than three days.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Primary adrenal insufficiency, stress dosing during a febrile illness (E27.1)</content>. He applied the sick-day rules correctly. With a fever below 39 °C, doubling the hydrocortisone is right; above 39 °C he would triple it. No sign of adrenal crisis: blood pressure steady without a postural drop, no vomiting, no confusion, sodium 135 and potassium 4.6 mmol/L, glucose 96 mg/dL. Fludrocortisone does not need to change. [S]<list><item>Hydrocortisone 20 mg on waking, 10 mg at noon and 10 mg at 4 pm while he has a fever, and for one day after it settles, then back to 10, 5 and 5 mg.</item><item>Triple the doses if his temperature goes above 39 °C.</item><item>If he vomits, cannot keep tablets down, or feels faint or confused: inject 100 mg hydrocortisone into the thigh and call 911.</item><item>Salty fluids; continue fludrocortisone 0.1 mg.</item><item>Nurse phone check tomorrow and the day after.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Diagnosis and Treatment of Primary Adrenal Insufficiency: An Endocrine Society Clinical Practice Guideline (2016); Influenza Antiviral Medications: Summary for Clinicians (2026)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Nurse phone check tomorrow and the day after. Six-week review as planned. Call at once or use the injection and call 911 for vomiting, fainting, confusion, or severe abdominal pain. Next scheduled visit 2026-03-09: six-week review after the dose changes: electrolytes, renin, repeat thyroid tests, and back pain. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
